00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:17.600
What about the ship on the surface? We might be able to salvage what we need.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.950
Any word on how much longer he's planning to stay in orbit?

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:24.640
The repairs are moving slow.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.910
That'd be the last to question the captain's orders, but doesn't it seem a little odd?

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.340
that we're staying here to save a nest of Xindi.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.110
The captain thinks it's the right thing to do.

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.650
We may be at war, but these creatures need our help.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.630
Meanwhile, their big brothers are trying to destroy Earth.

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.200
Every day we stay here gives them more time

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:45.200
to finish their weapon.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:50.460
Look, there's a chance we'll run into more insectoids

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:51.560
when we reach that red giant.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:55.190
Spending an extra day or two studying their tactical systems

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:56.130
might be useful.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:08.100
Ah, Captain, I've learned quite a bit about this fascinating fellow.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:13.020
Perhaps fellow is the wrong word. Their species is genderless.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.380
Who made all those eggs?

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:21.860
Produce asexually. Each adult appears capable of producing multiple egg sacs.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.380
I wouldn't be surprised if every insectoid vessel had its own hatchery.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:29.980
I'd estimate their lifespan is no more than...

